 public support CPI I can see  why you never made it in politics  because your 40 principle principle  listen now we've got time for a couple  of questions one of the things when we  put out the fact that you were coming on  one of our viewers wanted to ask how do  you remain so hopeful because you must  be helpful to have lived a life that  you've lived how do you remain hopeful  in in the face of tragedy and horrors  and also just the kind of things that  we've just been talking about you know  the political political system that  ignores the voices of many many people  how do you maintain a sense of optimism  and hope when all of that is happening  well I guess I'm an unreconstructed  1960s idealist yeah I grew up in here of  great optimism about what could change  and how we could change it and I've seen  so many changes in my lifetime which of  course inspires me to keep campaigning  you know I think about terrible  injustice as such as the systemic legal  persecution of LGBT people in this  country which is now history and I with  many thousands of others was part of  that process of change and I think to  myself wow I helped do  something there's made life better for  vast numbers of LGBT people in Britain  yeah I look at the global stage and  and I've seen so many dictatorships fall  in my lifetime there were still  dictatorships and even new ones but you  know Franco is history Pinochet is  history  Marco's is history the old Soviet Union  is history  you know these are momentous changes  which should be brought about by  millions of people organizing to fight  for that change and so I just I feel  inspired by the changes that I'd be part  of and feel hopeful that further change  can come and I know that if we put our  minds to it we can change things not  always and will not always immediately  but but ultimately no too many last  forever  yeah out of Hitler promised to one  thousand year Reich it lasted all of 12  years there are many others in power  perhaps not direct tyrants but you know  people perpetrating great injustice they  won't last forever they will have their  day and be gone and that just keeps me  one piece want to fight on and help  bring about that change we have to end  up there than wait what a wonderfully  uplifting beautiful way to end the show  um thank you very much for coming on  Peter we always ask the final question  which is what is a one issue that you  think is incredibly important but people  aren't talking about nearly as much as  they should be do you think it's  obviously we've covered PR is there  something else says particularly dear to  your heart for me economic democracy is  the elephant in the room we live in  Britain and Western countries in an  economic detailer ship where the rich  and powerful have all the votes it's not  one person one vote in the economy  the directors the major shareholders the  managers they have all the votes and  ordinary people are excluded and  disenfranchised so when it comes to the  running of both private corporations and  public institutions like the National  Health Service they are run on quasi  authoritarian lines top-down but people  who work in those institutions have  little or no power and that strikes me  as being totally out of kilter with our  political system in politics we expect  democracy we expect one-person one-vote  we expect our rich person to have no  more boats than a poor person but that's  not translated into the economy so my  ask is if we expect political democracy  why not echo my democracy too why can't  we have a system for example where  private corporations and public  institutions would be required by law to  have say one-third employee  and consumer representatives on their  management boards with full rights to  information and full voting rights to  make it a more coke or partnership  between the people at the top and the  grassroots and you've got to remember  that the people who work in both private  and public institutions at the  grassroots they have a vast wealth of  practical knowledge about how those  institutions work how they could be  improved lots of things but their voices  and their contributions not being heard  so it's not only an issue of fairness  it's also an issue of making those  workplaces institutions operate more  effectively and efficiently so you know  I'd love to see you know the grass roots  porters nurses and doctors in the health  service have a direct input through an  electro presentative or more on the  managing board of their NHS trusts so  they could take to that major ball  practical ideas for about proving the  service and to have patient  representatives as well to give their  voice to say this is how the hospital  needs to change its operating system to  make it better for patients and for the  delivery of care now the German Works  model the German works council model is  an example of that I'd go it further but  it's a good example and I say that in  Germany  first of all employees feel they have a  stake in the company because they've got  representation on the works council  board secondly when they have a  grievance they take it to the board and  often it gets resolved that I'd have to  go on strike so you're not losing  production through strikes your  productivity is better the company's  operation is more efficient and  employees feel better involved and  rewarded because so often when those  issues are taken to the works council  there may be some resistance and some  are geology but in the end often is not  there'll be some compromise and the  workers in the end will make a game that  doesn't have an improvement  yeah we're too polarized but it should  and in the 1970s under the Labour  government of Jim Callaghan there was a  report produced by Lord Bullock which  proposed a very similar system sadly  sadly the left and the trade unions shut  it down they said this is corporatism  this is like no nobbling the unions  that's madness imagine if trade  unionists or employees had direct input  to the match of boards their companies  they could address their grievances in  the boardroom not on the picket line  they could take their message direct to  the people who had the power they have a  vote and they could perhaps persuade  some of the managers and shareholders to  vote with them so to me it is so obvious  that we need some system  of economic democracy it's good for the  economy it's good for fairness and  social justice there we go interesting  all right well Peter thank you so much  for coming on we really appreciate you  spending this time with us if you  enjoyed watching this episode do follow  us on YouTube subscribe to our channel  give us a rating on iTunes if you prefer  to listen follow us on at trigger pod on  Instagram and Twitter and we'll see you  in the week's time from now thank you  very much for tuning in thank you thank  you  [Music]
 

